video:  Schadenfreude, German word, schadenfreude, right? It means to take great joy in the pain and suffering of others. Fun fact, that word was never translated into another language. I wonder why. My story happened in Edinburgh, Scotland. What I do whenever I'm traveling is, if I get to be in the same place as my friends, we try and spend as much time together and what we'll do is we'll order food or we'll go out and when we do that, we allow one person to designate the meal. And so whenever my day comes around, consistently, I pick Indian food, all right? Yeah, I think it's the best cuisine in the world, nothing comes close, you don't agree with me, let's meet in the parking lot, we can fight. I love Indian food, loved it my whole life. So we're in Edinburgh, I'm with my friends, they're like, where are we going? Trevor, what do you say? I'm like, Indian food. They're like, every time? I was like, yes, every time, Indian food. I'm like, no, it's the same. I'm like, then why do you keep asking me? If you know, you know, stop asking me. And I searched, I searched for where we were gonna go because I always try and find the most authentic Indian restaurant I can find. Like, I mean like authentic Indian restaurants. Do you know what I mean? So what I'll do is I'll go to a place and then I'll ask around and I'll ask people. I'll be like, where do you go? And then like, where the white people tell me, I don't go there. No. Now, the reason it had to be so authentic, just so you understand a little bit of me. I grew up in South Africa, as you know, right? Now, what a lot of people may not know is that South Africa has one of the largest Indian populations outside of India. And so I was lucky enough to grow up surrounded by Indian culture. In fact, one of my best friends growing up was a little Indian boy by the name of Thiessen Pillay. Thiessen would get a packed lunch from his mom every single day. Every single day, the meal was curry. I know this because every day, we'd sit down together. Thiessen would open his little lunchbox. He'd open the bread, look inside, and then he'd scream at the top of his lungs. He'd be like, curry! Damn it, mommy. I was like, oh, but Thiessen, is it bad? I don't even know anymore. I lost perspectives. Here, you can have it. And he gave me his curry sandwich. I bit it. And Toronto, there are moments in your life that will forever shape who you are. Moments that will define who you grow on to be. This was one of those moments for me. I put that sandwich in my mouth. I bit down, and my mouth came alive. My tongue discovered its purpose. I didn't know potatoes could pop like that. The flavors were bouncing around. My eyes opened wide. It was, I just, I was like, wow, I was like, curry! Thank you, mommy. It was perfect. And since then, I've loved curry. So, fast forward many decades. We're in Edinburgh, Scotland. Going out with my friends. We're choosing a spot. I choose Indian, authentic Indian. We get to the place, and it's legit. Like, it is legit. We walk in, and there's Indian music playing. There are Indian people eating. Indian cooks in the back, Indian waiters, Indian guards on the wall. I'm like, this is it. I'm home. We walked in, we sat down. And as soon as we sat, one of my friends, Steve, launches for the menu. And I was like, Steve, Steve, it's cool, man. I'll just order for everyone at the table. And he's like, oh, oh, that's fine, Trevor. I'll just, I'll order for myself, thank you. I was like, no, no, it's not about that, Steve. I just prefer to order for all of us, yeah? Right, you good? He's like, uh, I don't know why that's necessary. I think I'll just order for myself. Is that okay? I was like, uh, it's not, no, it's not okay, Steve. It's not okay, right? Because you're probably gonna say some shit that's gonna embarrass me in front of my Indian people, right? And so I just need you to back off for a moment. He's like, I'm sorry, what would I say that could embarrass you in front of Indian people? I was like, I don't know Steve and I don't want it to happen. Just let me handle this, okay? He's like, let you handle this? Trevor, I'll have you know that I've eaten curry for many, many years. I'm like, Steve, this is not the time. Just, let's not argue, okay? Let's not argue, it's not the time to argue. He's like, what do you mean? Why would I embarrass you, why? I was like, there's no, you don't need to know why. Let's just not do. He's like, no, why, why? All right? I wasn't gonna bring up race, but you did, Steve. And now that you brought it up, let's talk about it. Every time I take my white friends to an ethnic restaurant, they always say some shit that embarrasses me. Every single time, every time. Either they don't know how to pronounce the food or they'll say some random shit. They'll be like, can I get that one, the papadums? What do you call these ones? You know, they'll be like, can I get this one, but not spicy? Kill yourself! Can I get the curry, but not spicy? Kill yourself! How are you gonna ask for curry, but not spicy? Your forefathers went around the world killing people for the spice. Now you don't want it? Shut up and eat the spice, Steve. And he's there, he's like, Trevor, okay, I really don't understand where this is coming from. All right? I'm not gonna embarrass you. I understand curry. I was like, Steve, let's just leave it at that. I got you, okay? I got you. All right? I understand this way more than you. He's like, Trevor, I'm sorry. You're not more Indian than me. Trevor, I'm sorry. You're not more Indian than me. I was like, I'm sorry, what? He's like, yeah, you are not more Indian than me. I was like, Kuch Kuch Hota Hai. What the hell is that? Exactly. Don't tell me I'm not more Indian than you. You shut up. When the waiter comes in, you shut up. He's like, I'm not gonna shut up. I was like, you shut up. He's like, okay, fine. Order, order then. And the waiter walks over. This beautiful Indian man. He's got his beautiful Indian garb, and he steps up. And I know, Steve, I can see him questioning my authority out of the corner of my eye. I wanted to show him that these were my people. And as the waiter got there, I didn't even let him speak. I just jumped up and I was like, good evening. I'm going to be ordering for the entire table, please. Could we please have three garlic roll naans? We're going to have three Romali rotis. Give us one shahi paneer, one palak paneer. You know what? We're going to have a Rogan Josh lamb, of course. Could we also please have one butter chicken for my Caucasian friends? And then we're going to have three samosas and three mango lassies to wash it all down. Thank you very much. I crushed it. I could feel it. Everyone could feel it. They could feel something. Because the table went dead. The waiter just stared at me. At first I thought he was impressed. Then I realized he was confused. And this Indian man leans in. And looks me dead in the eye and he's like, sorry, sir, could you repeat what you said? I kind of hear what you were saying, right? You got a bit of an accent. And I was like, oh shit! And he's like, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no. He's Scottish! I mean, I know he's Scottish. I mean, we're in Scotland, he's Scottish, but it's like, he's Indian. I didn't expect that, right? Because the whole place is Indian. There's an Indian restaurant, the people eating, we're Indian, there's Indian gods on the wall. The guy was Indian, his beard was Indian, but his mouth was Shrek. I didn't expect that. And now he didn't understand me because I trudeaued too much. I didn't need to try so hard. But then I realized I also couldn't stop because that would be racist. So now I've got the Scottish Indian man looking at me. Everyone's quiet and he's like, if you don't mind, laddie, could you order again a little slower this time, right? And then I hear, what, yourself? You want me to place the entire order again? Ah, if you don't mind, I'm ready whenever you are. You know, I think everybody can order for themselves at the table. I don't think it's necessary for me to hold their hands. Everybody can order individually and we can go from there, okay? We can go from there. And Steve was like, oh, I wish I could, but I'm so afraid I might offend someone. I think my friend here should order for us. Please, Trevor, go ahead. Go ahead. You're far more cultured than I am. I said, no, no, no, no. Steve, please, you can dry your hand. I'm sure you know exactly what you want. He's like, no, but what if I say something embarrassing? The last thing I'd want to do is offend another person's culture, Trevor. Said, no, you can't offend anybody. There are no mistakes here, okay? We are all friends, okay? If anybody make a mistake, we forget about it. In fact, whatever happens today, we're all going to forget about it, okay? Just order, just order. He's like, no, I wish I could. I really wish I could. But Trevor, please go. I said, no, no, you just order, Steve. He's like, oh, please, Trevor. I was like, order, bastard! Just order! The waiter was like, hey, hey, hey, hey, there's no need to fight amongst yourselves. Look, clearly your friend's a little uncomfortable, right? Just order for him. Like, yes, he's totally uncomfortable. Okay, could we please have three Romali rotis and three garlic naan, one shahi paneer, one palak paneer, and could we also get a lamb Rogan Josh, and we're also going to be having one butter chicken. For your Caucasian friends! For anybody, for anybody. Anybody can enjoy different types of curry. They don't all have to be spicy. It doesn't matter, as long as you participate. There is no wrong way. Oh, you're so understanding, Trevor. Yes, yes, I am. Could we also please get the zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz. What, you're gonna answer that? Zzz, zzz. No, they can call me back later. Zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz. He's like, it's getting awkward, just answer your phone, I'll wait. Zzz, zzz. Okay. Zzz, zzz, zzz. Zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz, zzz. Hello? Yes, this is Trevor speaking, how can I help you? Yes, no, the one and the same. Can you get to the point? I can't talk right now, can you? Can you tell me what you need? I can't speak for too long, can you? No, no, no, there is nothing wrong with my voice. Can you just get? No, no, everything is fine. No, I have not been kidnapped, no. No, I have not been kidnapped. Everything is fine, this is not a secret message. Just, can you tell me what you need? No, we can talk later then. No, yeah, my voice is like this sometimes. Don't worry about that. Just wait. No, and I think I know my voice. I think I know my voice also. Yeah, well, I've been me my whole life, okay? I've been me my, okay, fine, fine. We'll talk later, we'll talk later. Okay, okay, okay. Love you too, mommy. Bye-bye. This is the most awkward meal I've ever had in my life. I barely ate a thing. I sat there thinking about my life. We finally get up to leave. Steve is beaming from ear to ear. They walk out ahead of me, and as I'm leaving the restaurant, the waiter waves us off. He's like, ah, good night, everybody. He's like, have a great night. And we wave at him, and he's like, hey, excuse me, before you go, hey, aren't you Trevor Noah? A comedian, right? I was like, yeah, yeah, why? He's like, oh, no, because I totally got you, Trevor. I totally got you, Trevor. I was like, wait, wait, wait, what? Wait, you're Indian? He's like, of course I'm Indian. Of course I'm Indian. All places Indian. I totally got you, huh? I said, well, I don't understand. He's like, you don't understand. I understand. You walked in. I said, look, Trevor Noah coming. Then you start talking with accent. I go, I also do accent. Then you were doing accent. Then I do accent. Then you were doing accent. You make joke. I make joke. Do you like joke? I'm like, no, I don't like your joke. I don't like your joke. I thought I was about to get canceled. You see this shit? He's like, it was amazing. You were sweating so hard. Not from the curry, yeah? That was so funny, Trevor. I was like, it was not funny. My friends were laughing at me. He's like, I know. He's like, I wasn't scared, man. Why would you do that? He's like, because, Trevor, I take great joy in the pain and suffering of others. It's called Shad in Brighter. Yo, Toronto, you guys have been amazing. Thank you so much for coming out. I love you all. Good night. ♪ I'm out my world, I'm out my world, world, world ♪
